Edit 2:
Based on the discussion so far, it seems like the ArrayList is not fully supported in Pex due to a lack of support in some methods. I've tried debugging the issue but I can't seem to find a solution or even a single resource that confirms that ArrayList is not fully supported in Pex.
Is there a way to work around this issue?
Edit 3:
I have done a bit more research and here's what I've learned:


*

*Pex doesn't support ArrayList as expected. For example, when I try to create a case where the array has 4 items, Pex seems to randomly decide to only have 1 to 3 items instead of 4. It also doesn't support IndexOf, Contains, Remove etc. methods, and they will return incorrect results.

*There seems to be a mistake somewhere, as there is an open Pex Github issue for this exactly issue (https://github.com/Microsoft/Pex/issues/1420).


I would love to know if there's any workaround or better ways to test ArrayList with Pex. So far, I've only found two articles discussing the different constraints/limitations of Pex in relation to other libraries:  
- http://xunit.github.io/docs/comparisons.html  
- http://www.codeproject.com/Articles/108737/8-Reasons-Why-You-Should-be-Using-xUnit-NET-over-NUn

Can I get some more guidance on this issue? And is there any known bug with Pex for this specific problem?
Edit 4:
Microsoft replied that the current version of Pex (4.2) does not fully support ArrayList or List objects in some methods, for example IndexOf, Contains, and Remove methods. Due to this fact, it is not safe to use ArrayList and List objects with Pex. They have mentioned this as a current limitation and working on ways to support these methods in the future. They do not have a specific timeline for when it is expected it will be fully supported. 

This indeed seems like an important limitation to consider when it comes to the testing of collections with Pex. 


A: It's common when testing with Pex to hit upon some limitations with the library it's been supplied with. The main limitation is how Pex handles mutable objects like ArrayList, Dictionary, StringBuilder etc. As per Pex's knowledge, these are not fully supported and it doesn't guarantee the correctness of generated tests. You are correct in saying that there are open issues like this one.
In your case, using Pex with ArrayList is not recommended at all. 
You can use Pex with .NET collections except those mutable ones. It's also common to use Pex with Dictionary<TKey, TValue>, because they are also mutable.
