And then:
//...
private ArrayList arrayList;
private int valueToTest;
private void PUT_AddContract()
{
   int expectedOldCount = arrayList.Count;
   int expectedRet = arrayList.Add(valueToTest);
   int actualNewCount = arrayList.Count;
   Assert.AreEqual(expectedOldCount + 1, actualNewCount);
}

private void PUT_RemoveContract()
{
    int index = arrayList.IndexOf(valueToTest);
    if (index >= 0)
    {
        arrayList.Remove(valueToTest);
        int actualNewCount = arrayList.Count;
        Assert.AreEqual(expectedOldCount - 1, actualNewCount);
    }
}

// ... same for InsertContract and SetContract

Also note that unit tests have no meaning on Pex generated data, but only on the values you provide in initial state.
But really, it's important to consider the usage and scenario of the function. ArrayList is just for test purpose, don't use it in production, unless you understand the implications.
You would be better off using a more specialized data structure if you need something like ArrayList in your production code.
This is not related to NUnit and Pex, these are just different tools. NUnit and Pex help each other but they are independent tools.

A: Your code is correct - you've got everything that you need. Pex is a mutation testing tool, and it will generate different test cases, in this case different values of x.
Your methods will be called for different values of x (provided you also have a parameterized PexMethod for different x-values), and for each value Pex checks that the Count property behaves correctly (that returned value is always one greater or one less than previous value).
You can also see the values of x in the GUI after Pex run, this can be very helpful in determining what values of x are being tested by Pex.
In your tests you're checking that the count of ArrayList is incremented and decremented by calling the Add and Remove functions. It assumes that Count property always reflects the number of elements in the list. For ArrayList in C# Pex cannot control the content of the list, so Pex only tests the count of items of the list. 
If you want to check other properties like IndexOf method or Contains method or the actual values in the list Pex cannot do because it can't control the content of the list. But it can help you find bugs in your application by checking other properties.   
Let me know if you need any help.
Thanks,
