A: The question was asked 3 years ago and there still isn't a direct way to do this using ArrayList in .NET. 
However, if you could use System.Collections.Generic.List<T>, you could perform this kind of state-based contract verification via unit testing. For instance, you can add an item to the list, and then check the Count property. Then remove the item and check the Count property again. You can assert that they are different to prove the behavior is correct. A similar concept could be used for Insert and Set methods. 
But if no, you'll need to create your own class (or extend one of the existing ones), where you keep track of the Count, the capacity and the indexes of the elements. Then you can test that every operation follows the appropriate contract. 
In short, the capabilities required to verify such contracts do not come directly from the ArrayList class, you might need to extend functionality to have access to these properties/states during tests. This could likely be done using Pex/StateDP, but this task would be quite complex.

A: There aren't any specific tools available as such for verifying such contracts using ArrayLists in .NET since the classes in .NET, such as ArrayList, don't allow the inspection of inner state at the level of individual field assignments. 

However, as mentioned above, Pex/StateDP or a custom wrapper for ArrayList objects can give you a kind of state-based contract verification. But it wouldn't be 'contract testable' itself (i.e., the contract is about how the object should behave, rather than just what it returns), but testing of expected state transitions.

It may make sense to consider wrapping your ArrayList logic in your test code, providing your own asserts that verify the state transitions are as expected. The result would be a contract-adhering piece of code, that might look something like :
public void TestAdd(ArrayList list, int item)
{
  // Initial count
  int initialCount = list.Count;

  // Add item and get new count
  int finalCount = list.Add(item);

  // Verify counts match our expectations
  Assert.AreEqual(initialCount + 1, finalCount);

  // Our test is happy if this assertion doesn't fail.
}

Where any test that modifies the array list (e.g., by calling 'Remove' or 'Insert') would also have equivalent "Assert" tests.

That being said, please note, the above approach would not verify the contract of the ArrayList class itself. It would verify the post-addition of an item, how the count has increased by one. For that, you would have to create your own set of tests, or potentially verify the contract of ArrayList's internal implementation (which is outside your control as it's a .NET class). Even using State-Dependent Programming (SDP) techniques might not be able to verify the behavior of ArrayList.

For such complex objects (ArrayList, Stack, Queue etc.), testing may need to be more granulated and specific to the methods and properties you are interested in. If you don't, you might be testing at a high level of abstraction and not catching all the potential edge cases, while having no knowledge about what is actually stored or its order of storage.

In short, verifying contracts (as in terms of state transitions) of complex .NET classes, like ArrayList, might require a fair bit of manual coding and testing.


A: Unfortunately, the Pex tool in Visual Studio does not directly support examining the internal state of a class like ArrayList. The Pex tool in Visual Studio is designed to test all public methods of a class, not the internal state. 
However, you can create a wrapper for ArrayList where you store the states of the ArrayList, then you can test/verify them (unit testing).
Sample code:
public class ArrayListWrapper
{
    private ArrayList _innerList = new ArrayList();
    private List<int> _counts = new List<int>();
    private List<object> _values = new List<object>();

    public void Add(object value)
    {
        _innerList.Add(value);
        _counts.Add(_innerList.Count);
        if (_values.Count > 0)
            _values.Add(_values.Last());
        else
            _values.Add(value);
    }

